virglrenderer: temps indexed at run time without an ARRAY declaration are arrays (#589)

What: a TEMP declaration without ARRAY that the shader still addresses with an
address register (TEMP[ADDR[0].x+18]) becomes one GLSL array from its lowest
indexed register to its last (vec4 temp18[2]); direct uses of those registers
say temp18[1], indexed ones temp18[clamp(addr0 + 0, 0, 1)]. The registers
before it, and every declaration the shader never indexes, stay single vec4s,
so a shader without indexed temps gets the same GLSL as before (the first pass
notes the indexed registers; the declarations change only when tgsi_scan says
TEMPORARY is indexed). An indexed write uses the address register it names
(addr1 for ADDR[1]), as reads already did, and is refused when its index is
not in an address register.
Why: the guest's Mesa sends such temps for a clip distance output written in a
loop that it does not unroll: virgl_tgsi_transform_instruction (Mesa 26.2.5,
src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_tgsi.c) moves each clip distance output into a
temp and keeps the indirect index, so gl_out[gl_InvocationID].gl_ClipDistance[i]
in a for loop arrives as DCL TEMP[18..19] and MOV TEMP[ADDR[1].x][ADDR[0].x+18].x.
Each such temp was a plain vec4, so the write "temp18[clamp(addr0 + 0, 0, 0)].x"
swizzled a float: the Mac refused the shader ("Swizzle of non-vector primitive
float", Apple's GPU and its software renderer, M1 Max and M4) and skipped every
draw that needs it; an indexed read gave one component in all four. The same
holds for vertex, geometry and evaluation shaders that write clip distances in
such a loop. Older than virgl-shader-index-clamp.patch (upstream wrote
temp18[addr0 + 0], the same error) and than the clip varyings patches.
Not fixed here: that same Mesa code copies the temps back to the outputs with
the declared register only (MOV OUT[1], TEMP[18]), so in the looped form the
second vec4 (distances 4..7) never reaches OUT[2]. That is the guest's Mesa;
those distances stay undefined after this patch too.
Upstream: not sent; vrend upstream has the same bug (an upstream fix would
declare such temps as arrays too). The Mesa part is for Mesa's virgl driver
(not sent; OmacVM posts nothing upstream without the maintainer).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c: the gate's control shader as the
guest sent it, the loop for 1..8 distances, a vertex, geometry and evaluation
shader with the loop (whole and separable), an indexed read and write without
ARRAY, an index in ADDR[1]: array text, compiled by the Mac's GL; an ARRAY(1)
temp and a shader with no indexed temp keep their text.
Tests/virgl/test-whole-program-shapes.c L rows: the looped control shader for
1..8 distances draws (refused before), each distance cut on its own reaches
the evaluation shader (distances 4..7 of the guest's loop: printed as info).

